[{"location":"guide/#Guide","page":"User Guide","title":"Guide","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"guide/#Installation","page":"User Guide","title":"Installation","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"guide/","page":"User Guide","title":"User Guide","text":"pkg> add AlignedAllocs","category":"page"},{"location":"guide/","page":"User Guide","title":"User Guide","text":"AlignedAllocs.jl targets Julia 1.11 or newer. Installing the package also brings in PrecompileTools, which precompiles cache line detection to keep load times minimal.","category":"page"},{"location":"guide/#Allocating-Aligned-Buffers","page":"User Guide","title":"Allocating Aligned Buffers","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"guide/","page":"User Guide","title":"User Guide","text":"The core allocation API exposes two constructors:","category":"page"},{"location":"guide/","page":"User Guide","title":"User Guide","text":"xs = memalign(Float32, 256)                  # default cache-line alignment\nys = memalign(UInt8, 1024; align=256)        # explicit 256-byte alignment\nzs = memalign_clear(UInt16, 128; align=128)  # aligned and zeroed","category":"page"},{"location":"guide/","page":"User Guide","title":"User Guide","text":"The align argument must be a power of two of at least 16 bytes. When omitted the detected CACHE_LINE_SIZE is used.","category":"page"},{"location":"guide/#Verifying-Alignment","page":"User Guide","title":"Verifying Alignment","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"guide/","page":"User Guide","title":"User Guide","text":"Use alignment(xs) to confirm the pointer boundary:","category":"page"},{"location":"guide/","page":"User Guide","title":"User Guide","text":"@assert alignment(xs) >= CACHE_LINE_SIZE","category":"page"},{"location":"guide/","page":"User Guide","title":"User Guide","text":"The alignment helper returns the largest power of two dividing the buffer's data pointer. Empty arrays may report 0.","category":"page"},{"location":"guide/#Multi-dimensional-Arrays","page":"User Guide","title":"Multi-dimensional Arrays","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"guide/","page":"User Guide","title":"User Guide","text":"Call memaligned to request aligned storage for dense matrices or higher-dimensional arrays without losing ownership semantics.","category":"page"},{"location":"guide/","page":"User Guide","title":"User Guide","text":"az = memaligned(Float32, 32, 8; align=128)\n@assert size(az) == (32, 8)\n@assert alignment(az) >= 128\n\nbz = memaligned_clear(Float64, (4, 4, 4))\n@assert all(iszero, bz)","category":"page"},{"location":"guide/","page":"User Guide","title":"User Guide","text":"Use tuple arguments or variadic dimensions interchangeably. The result is a reshaped view of the underlying aligned vector, so broadcasting and mutation preserve the pointer guarantee.","category":"page"},{"location":"guide/#Fixed-Size-Arrays","page":"User Guide","title":"Fixed-Size Arrays","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"guide/","page":"User Guide","title":"User Guide","text":"When you need a compile-time shape along with pointer guarantees, combine the package with FixedSizeArrays.jl and allocate aligned buffers directly via the fixed helpers.","category":"page"},{"location":"guide/","page":"User Guide","title":"User Guide","text":"using FixedSizeArrays\n\nfs = memalign_fixed(Float32, 4, 4; align=128)\n@assert fs isa FixedSizeArrays.FixedSizeMatrix{Float32}\n@assert alignment(fs) >= 128\n\nparent_vec = FixedSizeArrays.parent(fs)\n@assert pointer(parent_vec) == pointer(fs)","category":"page"},{"location":"guide/","page":"User Guide","title":"User Guide","text":"Call memalign_clear_fixed when you need the storage zeroed before wrapping it. Both constructors accept either variadic dimensions or a single tuple, mirroring the FixedSizeArrays API. The alignment helper works on the returned arrays because the dense backing store remains available through parent.","category":"page"},{"location":"guide/#Choosing-an-Alignment","page":"User Guide","title":"Choosing an Alignment","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"guide/","page":"User Guide","title":"User Guide","text":"Keep the default for cache sensitive SIMD code paths.\nRequest larger alignments when interfacing with hardware queues or libraries that require explicit boundaries (for example, 256-byte video buffers).\nOversized alignments can fragment memory; profile before defaulting to the maximum possible value.","category":"page"},{"location":"guide/#Memory-Ownership-Semantics","page":"User Guide","title":"Memory Ownership Semantics","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"guide/","page":"User Guide","title":"User Guide","text":"Platform behavior is consistent across releases:","category":"page"},{"location":"guide/","page":"User Guide","title":"User Guide","text":"Posix: memalign wraps posix_memalign and returns an owning Vector (own=true).\nWindows: memalign uses _aligned_malloc; the returned vector installs a finalizer that calls _aligned_free.\nZeroed allocations: memalign_clear preserves the vector then clears memory via Base.memset.","category":"page"},{"location":"guide/#Error-Handling","page":"User Guide","title":"Error Handling","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"guide/","page":"User Guide","title":"User Guide","text":"Condition Exception\nNon-positive element count ArgumentError\nNon-bitstype T ArgumentError\nInvalid alignment ArgumentError\nAllocation failure OutOfMemoryError\nAllocator returned misaligned pointer ErrorException","category":"page"},{"location":"guide/","page":"User Guide","title":"User Guide","text":"Wrap allocations in a try/catch if you need to recover gracefully:","category":"page"},{"location":"guide/","page":"User Guide","title":"User Guide","text":"try\n    buf = memalign(Float32, 1_000_000_000; align=128)\ncatch err\n    @warn \"Falling back to smaller buffer\" err\n    buf = memalign(Float32, 10_000; align=128)\nend","category":"page"},{"location":"guide/#Resizing-and-Mutation","page":"User Guide","title":"Resizing and Mutation","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"guide/","page":"User Guide","title":"User Guide","text":"Aligned vectors behave like standard arrays. Resizing may reallocate and thus change the alignment.  Query alignment(xs) again when the exact boundary matters after operations such as resize!, append!, or push!.","category":"page"},{"location":"guide/","page":"User Guide","title":"User Guide","text":"Continue to the API Reference for function signatures and details.","category":"page"},{"location":"starting/#Getting-Started","page":"Getting Started","title":"Getting Started","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"starting/","page":"Getting Started","title":"Getting Started","text":"the examples do not show the prompt \"julia>\" (makes trying code simpler)","category":"page"},{"location":"starting/","page":"Getting Started","title":"Getting Started","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(\"AlignedAllocs\")\n\nusing AlignedAllocs\nxs = memalign(Float32, 128)\nalignment(xs) >= CACHE_LINE_SIZE  # confirm alignment\n\nys = memalign_clear(Int32, 64; align=256) \ntypeof(ys) # Vector{Int32}\n# confirm that the memory is aligned as requested\nalignment(ys) >= 256\n# 64 Int32s, zeroed, aligned to at least a 256 byte boundary\n","category":"page"},{"location":"starting/","page":"Getting Started","title":"Getting Started","text":"using AlignedAllocs\n\nxs = memalign_clear_fixed(Int32, 32; align=128)\ntypeof(xs) # FixedSizeVector{Int32, Vector{Int32}} \n# confirm that the memory is aligned as requested\nalignment(xs) >= 128\n","category":"page"},{"location":"starting/","page":"Getting Started","title":"Getting Started","text":"using AlignedAllocs\n\nnvectors = 4       # this many equilength, isotyped vectors\nnitems   = 16      # each vector has this manyitems\nT        = Float32 # each item is of this type\nalign    = 256     # each vector is aligned to (at least) this many bytes\n\nvecs = memalign_vectors(T, nvectors, nitems; align)\n\nlength.(vec) # (16, 16, 16, 16)\ntypeof(vecs) # NTuple{4, FixedSizeVector{Float32, Vector{Float32}}}\nv1, v2, v3, v4 = vecs;\n\ntypeof(v1) # FixedSizeVector{Float32, Vector{Float32}}\ntypeof(v2) # FixedSizeVector{Float32, Vector{Float32}}\n# ..\n\nalignment.(vecs) .>= align # (true, true, true, true)\n\n# confirm the vectors are equispaced\nptrs = pointer.(vecs);\n[Int(ptrs[i+1]-ptrs[i]) for i=1:nvectors-1] # [256, 256, 256]\n","category":"page"},{"location":"reference/#Reference","page":"API Reference","title":"Reference","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"reference/#Constants","page":"API Reference","title":"Constants","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"reference/","page":"API Reference","title":"API Reference","text":"CACHE_LINE_SIZE::Int: L1 cache line size detected at load time, falling back to FallbackCacheLineSize.\nFallbackCacheLineSize::Int: Static fallback alignment (64 bytes).","category":"page"},{"location":"reference/#Public-Functions","page":"API Reference","title":"Public Functions","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"reference/","page":"API Reference","title":"API Reference","text":"alignment(xs::AbstractArray) -> Int: Largest power-of-two alignment dividing the array's data pointer (returns 0 for empty arrays).","category":"page"},{"location":"reference/#AlignedAllocs.memalign","page":"API Reference","title":"AlignedAllocs.memalign","text":"memalign(::Type{T}, nitems::Integer, align::Integer=CACHE_LINE_SIZE) where T\n\nAllocate aligned storage for nitems elements of type T and return a Vector{T} whose data pointer is aligned to align bytes. align must be a power of two at least 16.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"reference/#AlignedAllocs.memalign_clear","page":"API Reference","title":"AlignedAllocs.memalign_clear","text":"memalign_clear(::Type{T}, nitems::Integer, align::Integer=CACHE_LINE_SIZE) where T\n\nAllocate aligned storage for nitems elements of type T, zero-initialize it, and return a Vector{T}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"reference/#AlignedAllocs.memaligned","page":"API Reference","title":"AlignedAllocs.memaligned","text":"memaligned(::Type{T}, dims...; align=CACHE_LINE_SIZE) where T\n\nAllocate aligned storage for a multi-dimensional array with element type T. The return value shares storage with a vector allocated via [memalign] and is reshaped to match dims.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"reference/#AlignedAllocs.memaligned_clear","page":"API Reference","title":"AlignedAllocs.memaligned_clear","text":"memaligned_clear(::Type{T}, dims...; align=CACHE_LINE_SIZE) where T\n\nAllocate aligned storage for a multi-dimensional array and zero-initialise the contents before reshaping to dims.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"reference/#AlignedAllocs.memalign_fixed","page":"API Reference","title":"AlignedAllocs.memalign_fixed","text":"memalign_fixed(::Type{T}, dims...; align=CACHE_LINE_SIZE) where T\n\nAllocate aligned storage for a FixedSizeArray of element type T with shape dims. The returned array uses memalign and preserves the alignment guarantee for the underlying dense storage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"reference/#AlignedAllocs.memalign_clear_fixed","page":"API Reference","title":"AlignedAllocs.memalign_clear_fixed","text":"memalign_clear_fixed(::Type{T}, dims...; align=CACHE_LINE_SIZE) where T\n\nAllocate aligned storage for a FixedSizeArray of element type T with shape dims and initialize all entries to zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"reference/#AlignedAllocs.alignment","page":"API Reference","title":"AlignedAllocs.alignment","text":"alignment(xs::AbstractArray)\n\nReturn the largest power-of-two alignment that divides the data pointer of xs. Empty arrays return 0 because they do not own storage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"reference/#Supporting-Internals","page":"API Reference","title":"Supporting Internals","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"reference/","page":"API Reference","title":"API Reference","text":"The following helpers are useful when extending the package to other platforms or writing integration tests.","category":"page"},{"location":"reference/","page":"API Reference","title":"API Reference","text":"is_alignment_valid(align::Integer) -> Bool\ncheck_args(::Type{T}, nitems::Integer, align::Integer)\nzeromem(ptr::Ptr{UInt8}, nbytes::Int)\nconfirm_alignment(ptr::Ptr, align::Integer)\nalloc_error(err)","category":"page"},{"location":"reference/","page":"API Reference","title":"API Reference","text":"Continue to the Technical Notes for information on compatibility and implementation internals.","category":"page"},{"location":"#AlignedAllocs.jl","page":"Home","title":"AlignedAllocs.jl","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"#All-allocations-use-the-new-Memory-infrastructure.","page":"Home","title":"All allocations use the new Memory infrastructure.","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"AlignedAllocs.jl provides fast, cache alignment aware allocations for Julia vectors, matrices, arrays.","category":"page"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"{Vector, Matrix, nD Array}{T} are supported\nFixedLength{Vector, Matrix, nD Array}{T} are supported\nThe package wraps platform specific memory allocation primitives\nin a consistent, small, coherent and type stable API.","category":"page"},{"location":"#What-AlignedAllocs.jl-brings","page":"Home","title":"What AlignedAllocs.jl brings","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"#Julia's-Memory-type-(MemoryRef)-is-now-a-cornerstone-feature-of-AlignedAllocs","page":"Home","title":"Julia's Memory type (MemoryRef) is now a cornerstone feature of AlignedAllocs","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"Aligned memory is provided\nbacking a Vector{T} or an Array{T,N}\nbacking a FixedSizeVector{T} or a FixedSizeArray{T,N}\nAligned memory is obtained\neither uninitialized or zeroed\nwithout offsets into another memory object\nwithout copying from another memory source\nSystem local cache line size is determined during precompilation\nthis becomes the default alignment\nmutually and successively aligned sequences are available\ncontiguous fixed length vectors are sequentially aligned\nthis works where the sizeof the constituent vector is\nless than or equal to the alignment of the vector's start\nif the constituent is larger than the alignment, the alignment is increased","category":"page"},{"location":"technical/#Tech-Notes","page":"Technical Notes","title":"Tech Notes","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"technical/#All-Vector-methods-are-supported","page":"Technical Notes","title":"All Vector methods are supported","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"technical/","page":"Technical Notes","title":"Technical Notes","text":"length\nsort!, unique!, reverse!\npush!, pushfirst!, pop!, popfirst!, popat!\nappend!, prepend!\ninsert!, deleteat!, keepat!, splice!, replace!\nempty!, resize!","category":"page"},{"location":"technical/","page":"Technical Notes","title":"Technical Notes","text":"copy(), deepcopy(), similar() do not preserve alignment","category":"page"},{"location":"technical/","page":"Technical Notes","title":"Technical Notes","text":"","category":"page"},{"location":"technical/#Mulitsequential-Aligned-Vectors","page":"Technical Notes","title":"Mulitsequential Aligned Vectors","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"technical/","page":"Technical Notes","title":"Technical Notes","text":"vectors = memalign_vectors(T, nvectors, nitems_per_vector; align=<alignment of every vector>)","category":"page"},{"location":"technical/","page":"Technical Notes","title":"Technical Notes","text":"Above, vectors is an NTuple{nvectors, FixedSizeVector{T}}.","category":"page"},{"location":"technical/","page":"Technical Notes","title":"Technical Notes","text":"each one shares the same underlying buffer\neach one starting 128 bytes apart\nKeep storage alive for as long as you use the fixed-size views.","category":"page"}]
